answer.worked:
Let $V$ be the volume of liquid in the cylinder. Since the cross-sectional area is $4000\text{ cm}^2$:
$$\begin{align*}
V &= 4000h \\
\frac{dV}{dt} &= 4000\frac{dh}{dt}
\end{align*}$$
Let the rate of leakage be $k\sqrt{h}\text{ cm}^3\text{s}^{-1}$. Then:
$$\begin{align*}
\frac{dV}{dt} &= 1600 - k\sqrt{h} \\
4000\frac{dh}{dt} &= 1600 - k\sqrt{h} \\
\frac{dh}{dt} &= 0.4 - \frac{k}{4000}\sqrt{h}
\end{align*}$$
Let $c = \frac{k}{4000}$. Hence:
$$\begin{align*}
\frac{dh}{dt} &= 0.4 - c\sqrt{h}
\end{align*}$$

Using $c = 0.02$:
$$\begin{align*}
\frac{dh}{dt} &= 0.4 - 0.02\sqrt{h} \\
&= \frac{20 - \sqrt{h}}{50}
\end{align*}$$
Therefore:
$$\begin{align*}
\frac{dt}{dh} &= \frac{50}{20 - \sqrt{h}} \\
t &= \int_0^{100} \frac{50}{20 - \sqrt{h}}\,dh
\end{align*}$$

From $V=\frac{4}{3}\pi r^3$:
$$\begin{align*}
r^3 &= \frac{3V}{4\pi} \\
r &= \left(\frac{3V}{4\pi}\right)^{\frac{1}{3}}
\end{align*}$$
Hence:
$$\begin{align*}
A &= 4\pi r^2 \\
&= 4\pi\left(\frac{3V}{4\pi}\right)^{\frac{2}{3}} \\
&= (36\pi)^{\frac{1}{3}}V^{\frac{2}{3}}
\end{align*}$$
Therefore:
$$\begin{align*}
\frac{dV}{dt} &= -k_1A \\
&= -k_1(36\pi)^{\frac{1}{3}}V^{\frac{2}{3}} \\
&= -k_2V^{\frac{2}{3}}
\end{align*}$$
where $k_2=k_1(36\pi)^{\frac{1}{3}}$.

